Q: Usage of pointers A: <p>Ok, if you want to be a C engineer, you MUST to learn pointer.(Or maybe C++23 is better to you)</p>
<p>When I was a beginner <strong>I got stuck on it</strong> too!!!Let me use a simple project to explain <strong>&quot;What Is Pointer?&quot;</strong></p>
<p>Pointer is the address, and there are something in address(like int,float...).</p>
<p>if you use p, it's the address of RAM, and *p is the value that in the address.</p>
<p>if you just use normal variable, the system will just copy the variable's value and give it to you.</p>
<p>But if you use pointer, it change the OBJ's value.</p>
<p>variable can just use in a function, if you write a function and declare a variable in it, you CANNOT use the variable in int main().</p>
<p>So Why Not Just Use  int *p = &amp;variable?</p>
<p>Because like for(int i = 0...) You Just Need a Temporary value, So You Can Just use normal Variable.</p>
<p>You will learn struct later, if you don't use pointer, that will be very complex.</p>
<p>note: &amp; = the value's address can be a temporary pointer</p>
<pre><code>//A simple project
#include&lt;stdio.h&gt;//I/O input output printf
#include&lt;stdlib.h&gt;//malloc
struct pokemon{
    int level,hp,speed;//package the things
};//struct need &quot;;&quot;
void level_up(int *level/*pass by reference*/,int *speed, int hp){
    *level += 1;//you modify the address's value
    *speed += 80;
    hp += 50;//pass by value(system copy the value of hp and plus 50 in function, in int main() hp will still be 91
    }
int main(){
    printf(&quot;Here Comes Dragonite!!!&quot;);
    struct pokemon *Drag = malloc(sizeof(struct pokemon));//give pointer some space to access value
    Drag-&gt;/*Use Pointer to point at struct use &quot;-&gt;&quot;*/level = 50;//Drag-&gt;level is an int not pointer the pointer is point at Drag(memory address)
    int hp = 91;
    int speed = 80;
    int *spd = &amp;speed;
    level_up(&amp;Drag-&gt;level, spd, hp);
    Drag-&gt;speed = *spd;
    Drag-&gt;hp = hp;
    printf(&quot;Dragonite's level:%d hp: %d Speed: %d&quot;,Drag-&gt;level,Drag-&gt;hp,Drag-&gt;speed);
    free(Drag);//!!!!!important or You Computer will lag and you need to reboot your cpu, because memory leak will stuck your RAM
    return 0;
}
//Console: Dragonite's level:51 hp:91 Speed: 160
</code></pre>

Q: preg_match help required A: <p>When parsing predictable formatted text which has no optional leading components and doesn't require lazy matching (which regex otherwise affords), <code>sscanf()</code> can be used to isolate substring as individual variables or populate a flat arrat. Also <code>sscanf()</code> affords the ability to cast numeric matches as floats or ints. <a href="https://3v4l.org/3CcBs#v" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>$txt = 'Auction Type: BuyNow, Auction End Time: 09/20/2011 09:00 AM (PDT), Asking Price/Current Bid: $5, Number of Bids: 0, Domain Age: 1, Description: , Traffic: 0, Valuation: $0, IsAdult: false';

sscanf(
    $txt,
    implode(', ', [
        'Auction Type: %*[^,]',
        'Auction End Time: %[^,]',
        'Asking Price/Current Bid: $%d',
    ]),
    $endTime,
    $price
);
var_export([$endTime, $price]);
</code></pre>
<p>Or</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>var_export(
    sscanf(
        $txt,
        implode(', ', [
            'Auction Type: %*[^,]',
            'Auction End Time: %[^,]',
            'Asking Price/Current Bid: $%d',
        ])
    )
);
</code></pre>
<p>Flagging a placeholder with <code>*</code> ignores the matched substring.</p>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>array (
  0 =&gt; '09/20/2011 09:00 AM (PDT)',
  1 =&gt; 5,  // cast as integers type
)
</code></pre>
